  "query_preview": "..\nLatest satellite and radar data show mostly cloudy skies with\nisolated showers across the region. increasing convection is seen\neast of the coastal waters and will approach the islands during the\nevening, ushering in increased showers and thunderstorms later this\nevening, near midnight. Overall, an unsettled pattern looks to\ncontinue across the Marianas through the next several days, with \nthe islands seeing mostly POPs (Probability of Precipitation) in the\n30 to 50 percent range through the week. Tinian and Saipan will see\nsome brief periods of 20 percent POPs early in the week. Meanwhile, \nGuam and Rota will see periods of locally heavy showers Tuesday \ninto Tuesday night as a weak disturbance drifts by south of Guam. \n\nMarine...\nNot much change in the marine forecast. A mix of swells from varying\ndirections along with small wind waves will make for some choppy\nseas, though seas will slowly fall through the week from 4 to 6 feet\ndown to around 3 to 4 feet later in the week. Winds will remain\nmostly light to gentle through the week.\n\nEastern Micronesia...\nA weak, low pressure system is still located north of Kwajalein this\nevening with gentle to moderate westerlies across Pohnpei and Kosrae,\nbecoming light to gentle upon reaching Majuro. Scattered showers and\nisolated thunderstorms will be possible at all forecast points\ntonight. A dryer trend will begin for Kosrae tomorrow and by Tuesday\nnight for Pohnpei with only isolated showers expected. Gentle to \nmoderate west winds will continue into Monday at Pohnpei and Kosrae \nwith winds easing to light to gentle by Tuesday as the monsoon flow \nlifts northward. Southwest to west winds at Majuro may briefly \nincrease to gentle to moderate tomorrow before dropping back down and\nbecoming light and variable towards the end of the week. Combined \nseas of 4 to 6 feet will continue through early this week at all \nforecast points before possibly decreasing.\n\nWestern Micronesia...\nA special weather statement has been issued for Yap State. Heavy\nshowers and thunderstorms are expected through the middle of next\nweek, and gusty winds at times near these heavy showers.\n\nFor Palau, scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms will persist\nfor the next few days. The monsoon pattern that is currently over \nYap State is expected to move in to Palau around Wednesday. Showers \nare expected to be scattered through Thursday as the monsoon pattern \nmoves into the Philippine Sea. Seas are expected to remain 3 to 5 \nfeet with winds light to moderate.\n\nFor Yap, there will be widespread locally heavy showers and isolated\nthunderstorms. Winds near these heavier showers may be gusty at \ntimes. Models indicate a potential circulation that may develop as \nthe pattern moves westward. Showers may begin to wane as early as \nWednesday. Rain totals are anticipated to be 1 to 2 inches a day. \nSeas are expected to be 5 to 6 feet through Wednesday before \ntapering down to 3 to 5 feet for the latter half of the week. \n\nFor Chuuk, A surface trough interacting with some mid-level\nconvergence is producing sporadic scattered showers around the \ncoastal waters. These showers are anticipated to fill in and bring \nrain to the island through Tuesday. Once this trough move westwards, \nfairly benign weather conditions are expected to return for the \nremainder of the week. Seas are expected to be 4 to 6 feet through \nabout Wednesday, then drop by a foot for the later half of the week.",
